              "exact_quote": "Comparison operators are used for comparing values."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0316",
              "exact_quote": "The section begins with a discussion of comparison operators."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0317",
              "exact_quote": "Comparison operators are used to compare primitive values."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0318",
              "exact_quote": "Equality can be tested by checking whether two variables, such as x and y, are equal."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0320",
              "exact_quote": "The equality operator (==) compares two values and returns true when they are equal."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0324",
              "exact_quote": "The inequality operator ( != ) returns false when the two operands are equal."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0325",
              "exact_quote": "The expression x is not equal to y returns false when x and y have the same value."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0326",
              "exact_quote": "Java provides the comparison operators: greater than (>), greater than or equal to (>=), less than (<), and less than or equal to (<=)."
            }
          ],
